Model discrepancy, double counting, and sensitivity to assumptions (@met-model-discrepancy: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @metrology, #source "../sources/GUM-Models.lingua#r_3AAJPGTHAAF72R05SJ1VJ7HN46", #source "../sources/Uncertainty-NIST-1900.lingua#r_75WCVW8CJG4TAFD3330T87588E", #source "../sources/Method-Validation.lingua#r_5XXJY86AVZZ7XRT71EY7TRYDJY", #metrology "Reporting-and-use.lingua#r_1DX8JW7A1K3R87MJ4W9AYVJ52D", #electromagnetism "../../../physics/electromagnetism/measurement/Uncertainty-model-discrepancy-and-an-electromagnetic-claim.lingua#r_2KYWTMX0SHJYWAH69AQ9H3068X", #electrical-power-engineering "../../../engineering/electrical-power-engineering/planning/Uncertainty-sensitivity-model-error-and-engineering-claims.lingua#r_6X4V9ZDF411WG03JF8D8K5SE1Z", #thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer "../../../physics/thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/measurement/Uncertainty-discrepancy-and-the-scope-of-a-thermal-claim.lingua#r_0CQRV60W4KYW8JS1G7F5MYHA3J", #fluid-dynamics "../../../physics/fluid-dynamics/measurement/Separate-input-numerical-sampling-and-model-uncertainty.lingua#r_4RNG06S89GFF2HKSX4SSMNPWCK") { r_13PJ61YH29120EKV42ZT1SQTG3
**Read:** [Developing and using measurement models — JCGM GUM-6](../sources/GUM-Models.lingua#r_3AAJPGTHAAF72R05SJ1VJ7HN46), clauses 8.5 and 9–11, plus relevant annexes; [Simple Guide for Evaluating and Expressing Uncertainty — Possolo](../sources/Uncertainty-NIST-1900.lingua#r_75WCVW8CJG4TAFD3330T87588E), contrasting model and uncertainty approaches; [The Fitness for Purpose of Analytical Methods — Eurachem](../sources/Method-Validation.lingua#r_5XXJY86AVZZ7XRT71EY7TRYDJY), robustness and intended-use studies.

A measurement model may omit a physical effect, approximate it inadequately, or describe a narrower use than the one claimed. Model discrepancy is not automatically captured by input standard uncertainties. Adding an arbitrary “model error” term can conceal the issue rather than characterize it; investigate the physical and statistical evidence for the proposed term.

Compare theoretical, empirical, and hybrid descriptions. A linear calibration can fit one range while missing a temperature interaction. A first-order thermal response can fail when the specimen contains several relevant timescales. A laboratory validation result can omit variation that occurs during transport, sampling, or field operation.

Avoid counting the same effect twice. A long-term reproducibility component may already include changes in temperature, operator, and calibration. Adding separate independent contributions for those same changes requires a model explaining what remains uncovered. Conversely, a broad observed spread may not include an unknown common reference offset.

**Work:** take one completed budget and map every contribution to a physical effect and evidence source. Mark overlaps and missing conditions. Analyse a plausible alternative model or input distribution and quantify how much the final conclusion changes. Identify an experiment that would distinguish the alternatives rather than choosing the smaller uncertainty by preference.

Retain assumptions whose influence is negligible at the intended purpose, and explain that judgement with estimates or checks. When competing descriptions remain materially different, report the unresolved limitation and the scope of supported conclusions. Advanced metrology depends as much on this judgement and revision as on implementing a sophisticated propagation formula.

**Continue:** [Report a result so its uncertainty survives later use](Reporting-and-use.lingua#r_1DX8JW7A1K3R87MJ4W9AYVJ52D).

